---
/**
 * p36 · Panel, Raking
 *
 * The narrow one. 26 across against p35's 28, with 22 x 14 of glass and a chin
 * only two rows deep, so it stands more upright — a desk monitor rather than a
 * set. It carries on two blades that drop straight out of the underside and
 * flare a pixel a row into their feet, which is the whole reason the body could
 * afford to lose the deep chin.
 *
 * The light is Mozz's logic rather than Hozz's: one source off the upper left,
 * outside the mark, with distance from it doing the work. The tone boundaries
 * therefore cross the glass as arcs — the corner nearest the source is the
 * palest tone in the set, the far corner the deepest, and no boundary is
 * straight, let alone horizontal. The distance is measured slightly taller than
 * wide so the arcs sweep rather than sit as quarter-circles.
 *
 * The bevel agrees with that source: the inner edge of the top and left keyline
 * takes the light tone, the bottom and right the shadow one, so the frame is
 * lit by the same lamp as the glass.
 *
 * The face field is calmed exactly as in p35 — the raking field is flattened to
 * one value across the face box and blended back out — so the ZZ never sits
 * across three tones.
 *
 * 10 tones. The supplied face — facePathsAt, md, compact, gap 2, exactly 8 x 8
 * — at (16, 13), the middle of the glass: 7 columns either side, 3 rows above
 * and below. 26 x 24 overall, centred on (16, 16).
 */

import MarkFrame from '../MarkFrame.astro';
import { facePathsAt } from '../../../data/mark';

interface Props { size?: number }
const { size = 128 } = Astro.props;
---

<MarkFrame size={size} title="Plozz — Panel, Raking">
